---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GcpStrip from '../../components/GcpStrip.astro';
import Changelog from '../../components/Changelog.astro';
import LearningFlow from '../../components/LearningFlow.astro';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'linguistpro');
const d = entry!.data;
---
<BaseLayout title="LinguistPro" description={d.description} wide={true}>
  <p class="eyebrow mono small">Flagship product · {d.version} series</p>
  <h1>LinguistPro</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Turn articles, documents, images, audio, video and captions into an interactive Hebrew learning
    workspace — with niqqud, translation, audio, morphology, karaoke and review.
  </p>

  <div class="btn-row">
    <a href={`https://${d.domain}`} class="btn btn-primary">Open LinguistPro</a>
    <a href="https://linguistpro.kolosei.com/library.html" class="btn btn-secondary">Open the Reading Room</a>
    {d.repo && <a href={d.repo} class="btn btn-secondary">View on GitHub</a>}
    {d.statusPage && <a href={d.statusPage} class="btn btn-secondary">Uptime status</a>}
  </div>

  <LearningFlow />

  <video
    class="demo-video"
    src="/media/linguistpro-demo.mp4"
    poster="/media/linguistpro-demo-poster.jpg"
    autoplay
    muted
    loop
    playsinline
    controls
    preload="metadata"
    aria-label="LinguistPro demo — the Studio, Google Cloud and the Reading Room"
  ></video>
  <p class="small muted">The short demo shows the core workspace; the capabilities below reflect the current v3.11 series.</p>

  <h2>Bring the Hebrew you actually want to understand</h2>
  <p>
    LinguistPro does not impose a fixed course. Start with a pasted passage, a public article, PDF or
    DOCX, a photo, an audio or video file, or captions in VTT/SRT format. The Studio turns the source
    into a line-by-line Hebrew table while preserving where each row came from.
  </p>
  <div class="feature-pairs">
    <article>
      <h3>Documents and images</h3>
      <p>Extract the main text from an article, open DOCX locally, or use Gemini under your own key for PDF and image OCR.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Audio, video and captions</h3>
      <p>Transcribe uploaded media up to 3 hours / 300 MB, keep segment provenance and follow the source with karaoke-style row highlighting.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Long material without the wall</h3>
      <p>Long audio is processed in bounded windows and large tables render progressively, with visible warnings when timing cannot be trusted.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Retell at your level</h3>
      <p>Create an A1–B2 derived retelling and compare familiar-vocabulary coverage before and after. A saved original remains untouched; replacing unsaved editor text requires confirmation.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Honest limit: remote YouTube video without user-provided captions is not automatically downloaded or transcribed.
  </p>

  <h2>Understand every word without invented certainty</h2>
  <p>
    Tap a word for its lemma, part of speech, root, binyan, meaning and inflection table. A shipped
    offline Pealim dataset covers 9,279 paradigms. Context can disambiguate homographs, while provenance
    and confidence labels distinguish a decisive reading, a likely reading and an unresolved one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a deliberate product rule: deterministic sources establish morphology; AI may explain the
    result, but it does not silently invent a grammatical analysis.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading becomes memory</h2>
  <p>
    LinguistPro owns the learning loop with FSRS-6. A word saved while reading keeps its source sentence;
    when it becomes due, it can return inside real context rather than as an isolated flashcard. Reading
    Room and Studio share one memory trail, and Anki remains an optional companion through rich
    <code>.apkg</code> export with audio and review read-back.
  </p>
  <div class="feature-pairs">
    <article>
      <h3>One note, two surfaces</h3>
      <p>A saved word has one canonical personal note across Reading Room and Studio. Your wording remains distinct from the machine reference meaning.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Portable by design</h3>
      <p>Text-card-v2 archives preserve source, rows, segments and provenance. Account sync and library bundles provide separate continuity paths, with their scope disclosed before use.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2>The Reading Room</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/products/reading-room">Reading Room</a> applies the same engine to the public-domain
    Hebrew canon: <strong>26,455 works catalogued</strong> from Project Ben-Yehuda and
    <strong>796 prepared for interactive reading</strong> today. Browse by period and author, search,
    continue where you stopped, follow a graded on-ramp and ask for the next text just beyond your current vocabulary.
  </p>
  <p class="small"><a href="/products/reading-room">More about the Reading Room →</a></p>

  <h2>Local-first, with explicit choices</h2>
  <p>
    The primary workspace runs in browser-local OPFS + SQLite WASM. BYOK compute sends a selected source
    to the selected provider under the learner's own credentials. Optional account sync, research,
    Telegram/Mini App and connected-agent access are separate capabilities with their own consent and
    revocation controls — they are not implied by opening the app.
  </p>

  <GcpStrip services={d.gcpServices} planned={d.gcpPlanned} />

  <div class="status-note">
    <p class="eyebrow">Experimental integrations</p>
    <p>
      Consent-controlled Telegram learning and OAuth/MCP connections to external AI assistants have
      passed owner-live validation. They remain an owner pilot, not a claim of general availability or external product validation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Research mode</h2>
  <p>
    LinguistPro includes a separate opt-in cohort research mode supporting a Hebrew-ulpan pedagogy diploma
    project. The methodology is <a href="https://osf.io/zdv9j">preregistered at OSF</a>; participation and
    research data collection require explicit consent.
  </p>

  <Changelog entries={d.changelog} />

  <div class="btn-row">
    <a href={`https://${d.domain}`} class="btn btn-primary">Open LinguistPro</a>
    <a href="/technology" class="btn btn-secondary">How the technology fits together</a>
    <a href="/privacy" class="btn btn-secondary">Privacy choices</a>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
